  "raw_text": "92\n\n(\nEx/Ej\n(\nwqu\n-\n05/19\n):\n \nBaibil bukka kum uya kakaira apia sa kaka,\n \nyawan dîa sip\n \nsa daukaia?\n \n–\n \nIf a Bible book is not very well\n\nknown (to us), what are we able to\n \ndo?\n \n/ Si un libro de la Biblia no es muy conocido (para nosotros), ¿qué podemos\n\nhacer?\n)\n\nKakaira apia uplika kum\n \n–\n \na person not known, a stranger\n \n/ una persona desconocida, un extraño\n\nKakaira kaia\n \n(v)\n \n–\n \nto know, to be acquainted\n \n/ conocer, estar familiarizado\n\nKakaira takaia\n \n(v)\n \n–\n \nto get to know, to get acquainted, to come to know\n \n/ llegar a conocer, venir a conocer\n\n(Ex/Ej(\ndb-1Timoti 2:4\n):\n \nkasak tanka ba sin witin nani kakaira taki banhbia\n \n–\n \nalso their coming to know the truth\n \n/ también\nhan llegado a conocer la verdad)\n\n(Ex/Ej(\nmwb-06/20\n):\n \nMoses ba Jehova ra kau kakaira takaia want kan bara witin nina tânka ba sin\n \n–\n \nMoses was wanting to\ncome to know Jehovah better and also to come to know the understanding of his name\n \n/ Moisés quería conocer mejor a\nJehová y también conocer el significado de su nombre.)\n\nKakaira\n \n(2) (\nn-irregular agent form / s-forma agente irregular - kakaikra\n)\n \n–\n \nwatcher, looker\n \n/ vigilante\n\n(Construct:\n \nKakairika\n)\n\nAisi kakaira\n \n(n-agent/s-agente)\n \n–\n \nreader, person who reads\n \n/ lector, persona que lee\n\nMain kakaira\n \n(n-agent/s-agente)\n \n–\n \nwatcher, guard\n \n/ vigilante, guardia\n\nSîp main kakaira\n \n(n-agent/s-agente)\n \n–\n \nshepherd\n \n/ pastor\n\nPura kakaira\n \n(n/s)\n \n–\n \noverseer, administrator, director, supervisor, elder (in the congregation)\n \n/ superintendente,\nadministrador, director, supervisor, anciano (en la congregación)\n\nImpaki pura kakaira\n \n(n/s)\n \n–\n \ntravelling overseer, circuit overseer\n \n/ superintendente via\nnjante\n,\n\nsuperintendent\ne\n \nde circuito\n\nImpaki Pura Kakaira bara ai Maritka nani Skulka\n \n–\n \nSchool for Circuit Overseers and Their Wives\n \n/\n\nEscuela para Superintendentes de Circuito y sus Esposas\n\nKakalra\n \n(\na/t\n:\n \nKakalwra\n-\ndict\n) (\ndb\n-\n2\n \nTim\noti\n \n3:2\n)\n \n–\n \nbraggart, boastful person\n \n/ fanfarrón, persona jactanciosa\n\nKakamu\nk\n \n(n/s)\n \n–\n \niguana\n \n/ iguana\n\nKakamuk kati\n \n–\n \nMarch\n \n/\n \nm\narzo\n\n(Lit:\n \niguana moon\n \n/ luna de iguana\n)\n\n(\nNote/Nota\n: Some say it is this time that the iguanas\n \ncome\n \ndown from\n \nthe trees to lay their eggs\n.\n \n/ Algunos dicen que es\n \nen\n\nesta\n \ntemporada\n \nque las iguanas bajan de los árboles para poner sus huevos.\n)\n\nKakangbra\n \n(n\n-\nagent/s\n-\nagente)\n \n–\n \nmusician\n \n/ músico\n\nKakau\n \n(\ndict,dictu\n) (n/s)\n–\n \ncacao, cocoa\n \n/ cacao\n\nKakma\n \n(n/s)\n \n–\n \nnose\n \n/ nariz\n\n(Construct:\n \nKaikma\n \n–\n \nmy nose\n \n/ mi nariz;\n \nKakmam\n \n–\n \nyour nose\n \n/ tu nariz;\n \nAi kakma\n \n–\n \nhis/her/its/their nose\n \n/ su nariz)\n\n(Ex/Ej:\n \nKaikma nari sa\n \n–\n \nMy nose is itchy\n \n/ Me pica la nariz)\n\n(Ex/Ej:\n \nKaikma nari aikisa\n \n–\n \nMy nose is itchy\n \n/ Me pica la nariz)\n\nKakma bara bîla prakaika\n \n(\ntkl\n) (n/s)\n \n–\n \nmask, something to cover the nose and mouth\n \n/ mascarilla, algo para cubrir\nla nariz y la boca\n\nKakma trisbaia\n \n(v)\n \n–\n \nto sneeze\n \n/ estornudar\n \n(See also/ Ver también:\n \nTrisiam munaia\n,\n \nTrisiam daukaia\n,\n \nSmaya\nprukaia\n,\n \nSmaya pulaia\n)\n\nKakma unta\n \n(n/s)\n \n–\n \nnostril\n \n/ fosa nasal\n\nKakna\n \n(n/s)\n \n–\n \ndaylight, day, daytime\n \n/ luz del día\n, día\n\n(\nEx/Ej\n(\nmwb\n-\n12/18\n):\n \nAlmuk nani ba nit b\nâ\nra sa taim, kakna bara tihmia ra hilp munisa\n \n–\n \nWhen there is a need, elders help day and\n\nnight\n \n/ Cuando hay una necesidad, los ancianos ayudan día y noche\n)\n\nKaku\n \n(n/s)\n \n–\n \npelican\n \n/\n \npelícano\n\nKalat\n \n–\n \ncolor\n \n/ color\n \n(Alt:\n \nMapla\n)\n\n(Construct:\n \nKalatka\n)\n\nTaya kalatka\n \n–\n \nrace (of humans)\n \n/ raza (de humanos)\n\nKalik munaia\n \n(v)\n \n–\n \nto collect\n \n/ recoger\n\nKaliksan\n \n(n/s)\n \n–\n \ncollection\n \n/ colección\n\n(\nEx/Ej:\n \nKaliksan daukras\n \n–\n \n(We/they)\n \nd\non’t do collection\n \n/ (Nosotros/ellos) no hacemos colección\n)\n\nKalila\n \n(n/s)\n \n–\n \nchicken\n \n/ pollo\n\nKalila mahbra\n \n(n/s)\n \n–\n \nchicken egg\n \n/ huevo\n\nKalila wainhka\n \n(n/s)\n \n–\n \nrooster\n \n/ gallo\n\nKalkaia\n \n(v-trans; intrans:\n \nKalwaia\n)\n \n–\n \nto rip, to tear, to mangle\n \n/ romper, desgarrar, rasgar, destrozar",
